      "body": "## Summary\n\n`previewWithdraw` in both curve contracts reverts after just two deposits. The deposit path floors shares via `sqrt()`, so `totalShares` ends up slightly smaller than what the curve formula expects for the accumulated `totalAssets`. When `previewWithdraw` then squares that deflated `totalShares` (rounding down) and divides `assets / HALF_SLOPE` (rounding up), the subtraction `square(s) - deduct` underflows by 1 wei.\n\n`previewRedeem` is unaffected — it uses a different code path (`square(s) - square(sNext)`) that doesn't hit this mismatch.\n\n## Vulnerability Detail\n\nThe curve assumes `totalAssets = HALF_SLOPE * totalShares²`. Deposits go through `_convertToShares`:\n\n```solidity\n// ProgressiveCurve.sol:217\nUD60x18 inner = add(PCMath.square(s), div(wrap(assets), HALF_SLOPE));\nUD60x18 sharesUD = sub(sqrt(inner), s);\n```\n\n`sqrt()` floors. So after deposit 2, `totalShares` is 1 wei less than the exact `sqrt(2) * 1e17`. Meanwhile `totalAssets` tracks the full amount with no rounding.\n\nWalk through the numbers with SLOPE = 2e18 (test config):\n\n```\nDeposit 1:  1e16 assets  ->  shares = sqrt(1e16/1e18)*1e18 = 1e17          (exact, no error)\nDeposit 2:  1e16 assets  ->  shares = floor(sqrt(2)*1e17) - 1e17 = 41421356237309504\n            totalShares = 141421356237309504,  totalAssets = 2e16\n```\n\nNow call `previewWithdraw(2e16, 2e16, 141421356237309504)`:\n\n```\nsquare(s) = 141421356237309504^2 / 1e18 = 19999999999999999   (floors)\ndeduct    = divUp(2e16, 1e18)           = 20000000000000000   (ceils)\ninner     = 19999999999999999 - 20000000000000000              BOOM\n```\n\n1 wei short. Reverts with Panic(0x11).\n\nSame logic in `OffsetProgressiveCurve.sol` at line 160 — the OFFSET gives more headroom but the same drift accumulates over enough deposits.\n\n## Impact\n\n`previewWithdraw` is a core function of the bonding curve interface (`IBaseCurve`) and is publicly exposed through `BondingCurveRegistry.previewWithdraw()`. After just 2 deposits into any vault using `ProgressiveCurve`, this function permanently reverts.\n\nMultiVault currently only uses `deposit`/`redeem` internally, so the on-chain withdraw-by-assets path doesn't exist yet. But the function is part of the bonding curve's public API and the registry explicitly wraps it for external callers. Any contract or integration that composes on top of the registry's `previewWithdraw` to build an asset-denominated withdrawal (which is the standard ERC-4626 pattern these curves are designed to support) will hit a revert. Front-ends and aggregators calling the curve or registry to preview \"how many shares do I burn for X assets\" also break.\n\nThe bug is deterministic — it triggers on every `ProgressiveCurve` vault after normal operation (2 deposits). It's not a corner case or a dust-amount artifact. The `OffsetProgressiveCurve` has the same code pattern and accumulates the same drift, though the OFFSET gives it more headroom before it trips.\n\n## Code Snippet\n\nThe underflow line in ProgressiveCurve ([L155](https://github.com/code-423n4/2026-03-intuition/blob/314b7d4/src/protocol/curves/ProgressiveCurve.sol#L155)):\n\n```solidity\nUD60x18 s = wrap(totalShares);\nUD60x18 deduct = PCMath.divUp(wrap(assets), HALF_SLOPE);\nUD60x18 inner = sub(PCMath.square(s), deduct);  // underflows here\n```\n\nSame in OffsetProgressiveCurve ([L160](https://github.com/code-423n4/2026-03-intuition/blob/314b7d4/src/protocol/curves/OffsetProgressiveCurve.sol#L160)):\n\n```solidity\nUD60x18 s = add(wrap(totalShares), OFFSET);\nUD60x18 deduct = PCMath.divUp(wrap(assets), HALF_SLOPE);\nUD60x18 inner = sub(PCMath.square(s), deduct);  // same problem\n```\n\nThe deposit path that creates the drift ([L217](https://github.com/code-423n4/2026-03-intuition/blob/314b7d4/src/protocol/curves/ProgressiveCurve.sol#L217)):\n\n```solidity\nUD60x18 inner = add(PCMath.square(s), div(wrap(assets), HALF_SLOPE));\nUD60x18 sharesUD = sub(sqrt(inner), s);  // sqrt floors -> totalShares deflated\n```\n\n## Recommendation\n\nUse `squareUp` instead of `square` for the `s²` term in `previewWithdraw`. This rounds the squared term up (by at most 1 wei), which absorbs the drift and is also the correct rounding direction for a withdraw preview (more shares burned = protocol-favoring):\n\n```diff\n  UD60x18 s = wrap(totalShares);\n  UD60x18 deduct = PCMath.divUp(wrap(assets), HALF_SLOPE);\n- UD60x18 inner = sub(PCMath.square(s), deduct);\n+ UD60x18 inner = sub(PCMath.squareUp(s), deduct);\n  UD60x18 sharesUD = sub(s, sqrt(inner));\n```\n\nApply the same fix in `OffsetProgressiveCurve.previewWithdraw`.\n\n## PoC Setup\n\nRun with:\n\n```bash\nforge test --match-contract PoCCore -vvv\n```\n\nTwo tests:\n\n1. `test_submissionValidity` — full MultiVault flow: create atom, Alice deposits, Bob deposits, `previewWithdraw` reverts, `previewRedeem` works fine.\n2. `test_direct_curve_underflow` — calls the curve directly with 1e16 deposits, same revert, isolates the bug from fee logic.",
      "proofOfConcept": "// SPDX-License-Identifier: BUSL-1.1\npragma solidity 0.8.29;\n\n// HOW TO RUN:\n//   1. Copy this file into tests/PoCCore.t.sol (replacing the template)\n//   2. forge test --match-contract PoCCore -vvv\n\nimport { BaseTest } from \"./BaseTest.t.sol\";\nimport { console2 } from \"forge-std/src/console2.sol\";\n\ncontract PoCCore is BaseTest {\n\n    uint256 constant PROGRESSIVE_CURVE_ID = 3; // set by BaseTest: Linear=1, Offset=2, Progressive=3\n\n    // Full MultiVault flow: create atom, two deposits, previewWithdraw reverts.\n    function test_submissionValidity() external {\n        // sanity-check that curve ID 3 is actually the ProgressiveCurve\n        string memory name = protocol.curveRegistry.getCurveName(PROGRESSIVE_CURVE_ID);\n        assertEq(keccak256(bytes(name)), keccak256(\"Progressive Curve\"));\n\n        bytes32 atomId = createSimpleAtom(\"poc-atom\", ATOM_COST[0], users.alice);\n\n        // Deposit 1 -- Alice\n        resetPrank(users.alice);\n        protocol.multiVault.deposit{value: MIN_DEPOSIT}(users.alice, atomId, PROGRESSIVE_CURVE_ID, 0);\n        (uint256 assets1, uint256 shares1) = protocol.multiVault.getVault(atomId, PROGRESSIVE_CURVE_ID);\n        console2.log(\"After deposit 1  totalAssets:\", assets1, \" totalShares:\", shares1);\n\n        // Deposit 2 -- Bob\n        resetPrank(users.bob);\n        protocol.multiVault.deposit{value: MIN_DEPOSIT}(users.bob, atomId, PROGRESSIVE_CURVE_ID, 0);\n        (uint256 assets2, uint256 shares2) = protocol.multiVault.getVault(atomId, PROGRESSIVE_CURVE_ID);\n        console2.log(\"After deposit 2  totalAssets:\", assets2, \" totalShares:\", shares2);\n\n        // previewWithdraw reverts -- Panic(0x11): arithmetic underflow\n        // square(totalShares) rounds down, divUp(assets, HALF_SLOPE) rounds up, difference is -1 wei\n        vm.expectRevert();\n        progressiveCurve.previewWithdraw(assets2, assets2, shares2);\n        console2.log(\"previewWithdraw(totalAssets) -> REVERTED\");\n\n        // previewRedeem still works -- different code path, no underflow\n        uint256 out = progressiveCurve.previewRedeem(shares2, shares2, assets2);\n        assertGt(out, 0);\n        console2.log(\"previewRedeem(totalShares)   -> assets:\", out);\n    }\n\n    // Direct curve calls, no MultiVault fees. Shows the raw math breaking.\n    function test_direct_curve_underflow() external {\n        // deposit 1: 1e16 into empty curve\n        uint256 s1 = progressiveCurve.previewDeposit(1e16, 0, 0);\n        uint256 tShares = s1;\n        uint256 tAssets = 1e16;\n\n        // deposit 2: another 1e16\n        uint256 s2 = progressiveCurve.previewDeposit(1e16, tAssets, tShares);\n        tShares += s2;\n        tAssets += 1e16;\n\n        console2.log(\"totalShares:\", tShares, \" totalAssets:\", tAssets);\n        // tShares = 141421356237309504 (floor of sqrt(2)*1e17)\n        // square(tShares) = 19999999999999999,  divUp(tAssets, HALF_SLOPE) = 20000000000000000\n\n        // Panic(0x11): arithmetic underflow in sub(square(s), deduct)\n        vm.expectRevert();\n        progressiveCurve.previewWithdraw(tAssets, tAssets, tShares);\n        console2.log(\"previewWithdraw -> REVERTED\");\n\n        uint256 out = progressiveCurve.previewRedeem(tShares, tShares, tAssets);\n        assertGt(out, 0);\n        console2.log(\"previewRedeem   -> assets:\", out);\n    }\n}",
